# Topic 10 - per-fragment ownership.
#
# HONEST NOTE FOR THE DEMO: in a real org each line below would name a
# DIFFERENT team (@org/team-incident, @org/team-security, @org/team-app). On
# this single-account lab every handle necessarily resolves to the instructor,
# because GitHub requires a code owner to have write access to the repo and
# there is only one such account. The MECHANISM is real and reviewable - the
# routing, the required-review behaviour, and the per-path granularity are all
# exactly what you would ship - but do not claim on stage that three separate
# teams are being notified here. Say: "in your org these are three teams; here
# they all point at me, because I am the only member of this repo."
#
# The team each path WOULD belong to is named in the comment beside it.

# incident-response team owns the kill-switches
/infra/modules/waf-composed/rules/incident.yaml   @miroslavt-arch

# security team owns the standing security rules
/infra/modules/waf-composed/rules/security.yaml   @miroslavt-arch

# app team owns only its own fragment
/infra/modules/waf-composed/rules/app-team.yaml   @miroslavt-arch

# policy is owned by security — the app team cannot loosen its own leash
/policy/                                          @miroslavt-arch

# workflows and environments are platform-owned
/.github/                                         @miroslavt-arch
